window.__BLOG&&window.__BLOG('f1',"<h1 class=\"btitle\">Modelling the Human Teammate: Mutual Adaptation, Human Types and Hidden Traits in Human-Robot Collaboration</h1><nav class=\"btoc\" aria-label=\"Sections\"><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"0\">Setting: a robot that has to decide whether to give way</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"1\">Stage 1: modelling a person who might adapt (2017)</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"2\">Stage 2: types, libraries and human state (2019 to 2022)</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"3\">Stage 3: intent, task and style (2022 to 2025)</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"4\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"5\">Open problems, and a reading order</button></nav><div class=\"bbody\"><h2 id=\"b-setting-a-robot-that-has-to-decide-whether-to-gi\">Setting: a robot that has to decide whether to give way</h2>\n<p>Picture a robot and a person carrying a table through a room. The robot has a reason to prefer one of two routes (for example, its sensor sees the door on one of them). The person prefers the other. Somebody has to give way, and the robot has three simple options.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Insist.</strong> Always take the robot's route. The team gets the better route, but a person who wanted the other one may be annoyed, and people trust this robot less.</li>\n<li><strong>Always yield.</strong> Follow whatever the person does. Nobody is annoyed, but the team stays on the worse route (in one joystick study below, 15 of 17 people in this condition stayed on their own, worse goal in every trial).</li>\n<li><strong>Guess who it is dealing with.</strong> Some people will happily switch to the robot's route if it keeps pointing there; others will not move however long the robot waits. A robot that can tell the two apart can guide the first kind and comply with the second.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The third option is the opening move of this reading-map tree, and its gist fits in one sentence: <strong>keep an explicit model of the human, infer its hidden parts from what the person does, and choose the robot's behaviour from that estimate.</strong> Nine papers (2017 to 2025) follow this recipe with different hidden variables: how adaptable the person is, what <em>type</em> of person they are, what they are about to reach for, which task they are doing, whether they want to lead or follow.</p>\n<p>I should be upfront about the shape of this tree. It is not a clean line of inheritance like some others on the site. By my reading of the reference lists, only two papers reuse a component of an earlier member; the rest are connected by topic, or cite each other in passing as related work. So this post is a map of a <em>neighbourhood</em>, with one clear trunk (Nikolaidis et al.'s bounded-memory adaptation model) and several side branches. Where I group papers into stages, the grouping is my reading of what each one adds, not a claim about influence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-where-this-sits-and-what-is-hard\">Where this sits, and what is hard</h3>\n<p>This tree is the human-robot side of what the reading map calls <em>modelling teammates online</em>. Stone et al. posed the ad hoc teamwork challenge (an agent that collaborates with teammates it has never met, without prior coordination) in <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"AHT position · Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination\">their 2010 position paper</button>, and the <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"AHT survey · A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research\">2022 survey by Mirsky et al.</button> organises the field. In that vocabulary, five members here are <em>type-based reasoning</em> (both mutual-adaptation papers, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">ABPS</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"HOTSPOT · HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams\">HOTSPOT</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Lead or follow · To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Lead or follow</button>) and one is <em>goal recognition</em> (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Approx. Bayes</button>); the rest are planning or survey papers. The sibling line that prepares agents for unseen teammates by training against large populations is covered in its own post; here the teammate is a person, and the agent learns about that person <em>while working with them</em>.</p>\n<p>Three things make a person a harder teammate than a scripted partner:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hidden and possibly changing.</strong> Skill, stamina, willingness to follow and intent are not visible. They have to be inferred from a few actions, and they can change mid-task.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong guesses cost twice.</strong> A robot that misjudges the person wastes time and, worse, loses their trust. Two of the experiments below measure trust for exactly this reason.</li>\n<li><strong>People go off-script.</strong> Plan-recognition methods that match behaviour to a library of plans work until someone does something unexpected, which is the starting complaint of the <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"72\" title=\"Human modelling · Human modeling for human–robot collaboration\">Human modelling</button> survey.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Earlier work by Nikolaidis and colleagues (outside this tree) is the immediate background: cross-training (2013) taught a robot a partner's preferences, a 2015 paper clustered people into human types, and a 2016 paper proposed the bounded-memory model that the first two papers below develop.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-a-shared-loop-and-some-vocabulary\">A shared loop, and some vocabulary</h3>\n<p>Let's define the loop the papers share. A hidden human trait <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> (adaptability, type, intent, ...) is tracked by a belief <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_t(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. When the robot sees the person's action <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_H</annotation></semantics></math></span> in situation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, it updates by Bayes' rule and then picks its own action from the new belief:</p>\n<div class=\"mdisp\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>∝</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_{t+1}(\\theta) \\;\\propto\\; P(a_H \\mid s_t, \\theta)\\, b_t(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<p>This is a simplification: <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 · Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams\">Mutual adapt. '21</button> skips the explicit human model altogether, and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Approx. Bayes</button> approximates the update by simulation. But \"observe, update a belief, act\" is what the notes of this tree identify as the common loop, and the papers differ mainly in what <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> is. A few terms recur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adaptability <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span></strong>: the chance that a person switches to the robot's way of doing the task rather than keeping their own. <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a person who always follows the robot's lead; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> insists.</li>\n<li><strong>Mode</strong>: a way of doing the task, such as heading for goal A. The person is assumed to be in one mode, and to occasionally switch to the robot's.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounded memory <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></strong>: the person reacts to what happened in the last <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> steps only, not the full history.</li>\n<li><strong>MOMDP</strong>: a planning model in which part of the state is observable and part (here, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>) is hidden. It is solved offline into a policy that maps beliefs to robot actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Human type</strong>: a long-term trait bundle, such as skill or stamina.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy library</strong>: a set of pre-built robot strategies. Some of the papers below choose among them rather than plan from scratch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-tree-in-one-table\">The tree in one table</h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Papers</th>\n<th>Hidden variable</th>\n<th>How the robot acts on it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adaptation models (2017)</td>\n<td><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Mutual adapt. '17</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"70\" title=\"Shared autonomy · Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy\">Shared autonomy</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"72\" title=\"Human modelling · Human modeling for human–robot collaboration\">Human modelling</button></td>\n<td>Adaptability alpha (the survey: none)</td>\n<td>Solve a MOMDP: guide or comply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Types and human state (2019 to 2022)</td>\n<td><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">ABPS</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 · Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams\">Mutual adapt. '21</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"76\" title=\"Failure antic. · Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Failure antic.</button></td>\n<td>Human type; closest library policy; capacity, knowledge, motivation</td>\n<td>Pick a library policy; play its best partner; set planner costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intent, task and style (2022 to 2025)</td>\n<td><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Approx. Bayes</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"HOTSPOT · HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams\">HOTSPOT</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Lead or follow · To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Lead or follow</button></td>\n<td>Reach target; hidden task; follow preference and error-proneness</td>\n<td>Time the reach; ask when unsure; allocate subtasks by integer program</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"bfig\"><img src=\"assets/img/f1_method.webp\" alt=\"Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The figure shows the lineage that exists, and what to notice is how sparse it is. By the tree's edge list the inheritance links are two: the 2017 IJRR paper to the 2025 T-RO paper, and ABPS to the failure-anticipation planner. Everything else hangs off by topic or by a related-work mention.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-stage-1-modelling-a-person-who-might-adapt-2017\">Stage 1: modelling a person who might adapt (2017)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-mutual-adapt-17-the-model-at-the-root-of-the-tru\">Mutual adapt. '17: the model at the root of the trunk</h3>\n<p>Nikolaidis, Hsu and Srinivasa's <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">IJRR paper</button> is the origin of the thread, and the paper to read first. It is the journal extension of the 2016 conference paper and adds a hallway-crossing task, plus experiments on repeated trials and on transfer across tasks.</p>\n<p>The model is the <strong>Bounded-memory Adaptation Model (BAM)</strong>. The person follows a mode. At each step they switch to the robot's mode with probability <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> and keep their own with probability <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, deciding from features of the last <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span> steps (the most frequent human and robot modes in that window). <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> is fixed per person and discretised to five levels. A small chance of a random action smooths the model. Because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> is hidden, the robot's problem is a MOMDP: the observable part is the task step plus recent modes, the hidden part is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For the table-carrying task that is 734 × 5 = 3,670 states (hallway: 340 × 5 = 1,700), solved offline with the point-based solver SARSOP and executed online.</p>\n<p>The resulting behaviour is the \"guess who it is\" robot from the opening. It heads for its preferred goal and keeps going until it is confident the person is non-adaptable, and then adapts to them. A simulated fully adaptable person (<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>) switches after one step; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is recognised after two; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 0.75</annotation></semantics></math></span> takes several. There is no explicit \"information-gathering\" action: whether to disagree is decided by the policy from the current estimate, the two goal rewards and the discount factor.</p>\n<p>The main study had 69 online participants in three conditions. In the table-carrying task, 57% of people adapted to the robot with the mutual-adaptation policy against 26% with the cross-training baseline (chi-square test, N = 46, p = 0.036; the bar counts of about 13 of 23 and 6 of 23 are my reading of a chart, the text gives only percentages). Trust was higher than with a fixed robot (p = 0.048), and the policy was not worse than cross-training on trust and collaboration items under a non-inferiority test. Two follow-ups temper the story. In repeated trials (43 people) only 13% of non-adapters switched later, and across tasks (58 people) adapting in the first task predicted adapting in the second, but weakly (Nagelkerke <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> = 0.119). A hallway-only study with 65 people saw 48% adapting.</p>\n<p>The caveats are the paper's own. It is an online video study with a button interface and short interactions; the baseline comparison covers adaptation and ratings, not fluency; modes are assumed observable from one action; and the authors note BAM may not suit people who reflect on long histories or on the other's beliefs. A <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> pilot with 30 participants misread 23% of samples as adaptable, which is why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span> was used.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-shared-autonomy-the-same-idea-with-a-joystick\">Shared autonomy: the same idea with a joystick</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"70\" title=\"Shared autonomy · Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy\">Nikolaidis, Zhu, Hsu and Srinivasa (HRI 2017)</button> moves the model to a different setting: a person uses a joystick in a table-clearing task, and only the <em>robot</em> moves the world, so the person's input is noisy evidence of a goal rather than a state change. The MOMDP infers goal and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, uses memory <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and five <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> levels (520 states), and adds a <strong>disagreement penalty</strong> <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = -0.32</annotation></semantics></math></span> against goal rewards of 11 (the robot's better goal) and 10 (the other).</p>\n<p>With 51 participants and three trials each, 12 of 16 people (75%) in the mutual-adaptation condition guided the robot to the better goal at least once, and 9 of 16 (56%) did so in the last trial. In the one-way condition, where the robot follows the user, only 2 of 17 changed goals. The fixed-goal robot scores best on team reward by design; mutual adaptation beats one-way (p = 0.001) and is trusted more than the fixed robot (p = 0.010). Caveats from the notes: one task with two goals, generalisation is argued rather than tested, and the size of the no-adaptation group is not stated. It is a sibling of the IJRR paper, not a child: by my check neither cites the other.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-human-modelling-the-map-behind-the-methods\">Human modelling: the map behind the methods</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"72\" title=\"Human modelling · Human modeling for human–robot collaboration\">Hiatt et al. (IJRR 2017)</button> is the odd member: a survey with no experiments, numbers or method. It sorts models of humans by Marr's three levels (computational: what people do; algorithmic: how they think, including errors and timing; implementational: how that is physically realised) and compares them on four criteria: handling of noisy human processing, timescale, hand-coded versus learned structure, and training data. By my count from its figures it covers about 15 approaches (the paper gives no count). Its findings are loose but useful: models that capture human error tend to be narrow and short-range (\"the biggest downside\" of algorithmic models), models robust to noisy observations work best over long times, and combining levels (for example an HMM whose states are cognitive models) is recommended but untested. The authors call their selection somewhat subjective. In this tree it matters mostly as background: <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">ABPS</button> cites it for the point that human actions can be imperfect and that modelling many behaviours is costly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-stage-2-types-libraries-and-human-state-2019-to-\">Stage 2: types, libraries and human state (2019 to 2022)</h2>\n<p>The three papers here drop BAM's continuous-ish <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> for something coarser and easier to act on: a <em>type</em> the robot can respond to with a pre-built policy, or a <em>state</em> it can feed to a planner.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-abps-choose-from-a-library-by-inferring-the-type\">ABPS: choose from a library by inferring the type</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">Görür, Rosman and Albayrak's ABPS (AAMAS 2019)</button> builds on Bayesian Policy Reuse and the authors' own POMDP model of a collaborative robot, with the Hiatt survey as background only. Offline, 20 robot policies are solved from randomly varied models, and 16 hand-made human types are simulated (20 policies × 16 types × 50 tasks = 16,000 interactions). Online, after each task the robot updates a belief over types from six boolean signals (simulated activity recognition) and picks the next policy by <em>expected improvement</em>.</p>\n<p>The evidence is entirely in simulation. In the harder experiment (10 unseen human types, the type switching every 30 tasks, 300 tasks per scenario), ABPS reaches a discounted reward of 4.62 against 3.32 for the best single policy (+39.2%), with 31 warnings against 75.2 (−58.8%), success 0.92 against 0.84, and task time 26.04 s against 30.49 s (all p &lt; 0.0001 by ANOVA). In the single-human experiment it matches the best policy's regret after about six tasks. Read these against their baseline: the best single policy <em>chosen in hindsight</em>, which is a fair but modest comparison. The paper lists real users as future work, and the \"total warnings\" label in Table 1 may be a per-scenario mean.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-mutual-adapt-21-no-human-model-just-the-nearest-\">Mutual adapt. '21: no human model, just the nearest policy</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 · Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams\">Li et al. (IEEE THMS 2021)</button> shares the name \"mutual adaptation\" and a research programme, but not the mechanism. In Team Space Fortress the human and agent play two interdependent roles, bait and shooter, and the paper learns no human model. A library holds 13 bait and 11 shooter policies (reinforcement learning, a rule-based mirror, imitation), and a self-play table records how well each pairs with each other. During play the human's recent moves are scored against every library policy by cross-entropy; the closest policy is found and <em>its best partner</em> is played. The assumptions are that the library covers the human's way of playing and that similar policies earn similar team scores.</p>\n<p>Experiment 1 (104 online players) supports the second assumption: closeness to the best partner correlates with team score at r = 0.636 for baits and r = 0.834 for shooters. In Experiment 2 (134 players, 15 trials), with a human shooter and an agent bait, the adaptive agent beat random partner assignment (t(25.8) = 2.87, p = .008) but not the fixed one (p = .122), and trials 6 to 8 were higher than both (p &lt; .01). With the human as bait and the agent as shooter there was no significant difference. The headline \"42.3%\" is the one number I cannot pin down: the paper does not say which team type or baseline score it is measured against. Group sizes after cleaning were small (about 10 to 20), and the text says 12 policies once where its Table I shows 13 + 11. It cites both <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">ABPS</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Mutual adapt. '17</button> as related work only; nothing is reused.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-failure-antic-put-the-human-s-state-in-the-plann\">Failure antic.: put the human's state in the planner</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"76\" title=\"Failure antic. · Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Izquierdo-Badiola et al. (ICRA 2022)</button> takes a different route: the planner, not a policy library, should know the human's state. It borrows ABPS's capacity, distracted and tired components (merged into one motivation cost) plus a knowledge component from other work, and turns three scores per agent, each from 0 to 10, into PDDL action costs. A plan controller watches for state changes and replans <em>before</em> the human's action fails; the human's success probability is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mtext>max cost</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - \\text{cost}/\\text{max cost}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>\n<p>In a simulated two-box task, with 40 runs per case, success against a human who never contributes rises from 25.00% to 100.00%, failed actions fall from 44.5% to 8.5%, and replans go from 3.75 to 0.93 (as tabulated); with a human who contributes at random, success goes from 37.5% to 95.0%. Part of the gain comes from giving the human less work (the share of actions given to the human falls from 65.6% to 31.9% in the first case). Human states and failures are simulated, not sensed, only one scenario is tested, no other planner is compared, and no variances or significance tests are reported. It cites <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Mutual adapt. '17</button> only to say that such work chooses among predefined policies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-stage-3-intent-task-and-style-2022-to-2025\">Stage 3: intent, task and style (2022 to 2025)</h2>\n<p>The last three papers pick new hidden variables. Only one of them inherits from the trunk.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-approx-bayes-real-time-reach-intent\">Approx. Bayes: real-time reach intent</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Felip, Gonzalez-Aguirre and Nachman (Intel Labs, IROS 2022)</button> is the only member that predicts physical reaching, and it cites no other member. It infers where a hand is heading by <strong>Approximate Bayesian Computation</strong>: sample a candidate target from a prior (near the human, along the gaze, at detected objects), simulate a 3 s reach with a 9-DoF arm model, and keep targets whose simulated path is close to the observed one. To make this real-time a small neural surrogate, trained on 10,000 simulated paths (mean error 1.7 cm), evaluates 9,100 samples in 0.01 s instead of 1,365 s, and the grid is cached.</p>\n<p>On a task where a UR5e robot and a human pack 16 cubes into one box, task time is 36.49 s with intent prediction against 40.69 s for a turn-taking baseline (about 10% shorter, my arithmetic), with 57.05 s for the human alone and 64.55 s for the robot alone (10 runs per method). The number of human participants is not stated, there is no significance test, the plus-minus values are not defined, and the paper does not say how \"enough certainty\" to start a reach is thresholded. Its assumptions (a known reach simulator, a motion-capture workspace, one 3D target, turn-based pick and place) are my reading, since there is no explicit section.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-hotspot-infer-the-hidden-task-and-ask\">HOTSPOT: infer the hidden task, and ask</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"HOTSPOT · HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams\">Ribeiro et al.'s HOTSPOT (PLOS ONE 2024)</button> is an ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams. It builds on ad hoc teamwork work (the authors' earlier paper, Melo and Sardinha, and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"5\" title=\"PLASTIC · Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates\">PLASTIC</button>) and mentions Mutual adapt. '21 in related work only. The robot knows a library of possible tasks but not which one the human is doing; sensing is partial and a spoken reply may never come. A Bayesian estimator keeps a belief over states and tasks, and planning mixes a task Q-function and an information-gathering Q-function, weighted by one minus the belief entropy and the entropy respectively. When the robot is unsure it asks a spoken question (\"where are you?\"); speech-to-text, entity recognition and node identification form the sensing side.</p>\n<p>In Predator-Prey (exact values, 32 trials, 626 states) the mean number of steps is 6.75 for an optimal agent with full view, 10.22 for an optimal agent that knows the task, 12.84 for HOTSPOT and 76.06 for random; the gap between 10.22 and 12.84 is not significant (p &gt; 0.05). On the real-robot Toxic Waste task (9 trials with 7 lab members plus 32 simulated), the values I can give are approximate readings of bars: about 12 steps for HOTSPOT with real humans and about 13 in simulation, against about 10 optimal and about 63 random, and about 39 versus 13 steps without the locate-human query (p = 0.000656). Live speech accuracy was 58.62% for recognition, 77.77% for entities and 74.07% for nodes. The caveats are the usual small human sample, turn-based play, participants who were told the rules, and tabular methods that will not scale; the acronym TEBOPA is never expanded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-lead-or-follow-the-trunk-returns\">Lead or follow: the trunk returns</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Lead or follow · To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Noormohammadi-Asl, Smith and Dautenhahn (T-RO 2025)</button> is the one paper that clearly inherits from another member. Its Section IV-B adopts the bounded-memory model and belief update of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Mutual adapt. '17</button>, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span> again, but applies them to <em>two</em> hidden traits, each on 11 values: a following preference <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_f</annotation></semantics></math></span> (0 leads, 1 follows) and an error-proneness <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_e</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Those traits set costs in a <strong>min-max integer program</strong>: the allocation minimises the larger of the two agents' total costs, and a second program schedules the subtasks (solved with Gurobi). Directing a person who likes to lead costs extra; leaving an error-prone person unguided costs extra. The planner is a decomposition, so it does not guarantee a joint optimum.</p>\n<p>The human study (48 analysed participants out of 58 recruited, 144 tasks, a block task with a Fetch robot) is qualitative-heavy. In the hardest pattern the following estimate is higher (H(2) = 6.5, p = 0.039) and the robot assigns more subtasks (p = 0.042). In simulation (15 runs per combination), with a person who prefers to lead (follow probability 0.3), wrong human actions at a true error rate of 0.8 fall from 10.93 non-adaptive to 9.2 adaptive, and subtasks given to the human at error rate 0.1 fall from 8.93 to 6.8; at a follow probability of 0.9 there is no significant difference. There is no single headline percentage. The paper also reports no real-participant comparison against a non-adaptive robot, the participants are mostly young students (24.0 ± 3.9 years), and the counts of its interview groups disagree between the figure (15, 19, 4, 3, plus 3 and 1, so 45) and the text (17, 20, 4, 3) and with 48 participants; I could not resolve this.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-progression-in-one-line\">The progression in one line</h3>\n<p>Adaptability <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> for guide-or-comply (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Mutual adapt. '17</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"70\" title=\"Shared autonomy · Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy\">Shared autonomy</button>) → human types and a policy library (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">ABPS</button>) or the closest library policy (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 · Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams\">Mutual adapt. '21</button>) → human state as planner costs (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"76\" title=\"Failure antic. · Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Failure antic.</button>) → reach intent, hidden task and lead/follow style (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Approx. Bayes</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"HOTSPOT · HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams\">HOTSPOT</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Lead or follow · To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Lead or follow</button>). The last arrow is partly a loop: the 2025 paper goes back to the 2017 belief update. Nothing in the evidence says the later papers <em>replace</em> earlier ones; each picks a different hidden variable for a different task.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-how-the-line-is-measured-and-why-the-numbers-do-\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</h2>\n<p>Eight of the nine papers have experiments, on eight different testbeds: a joystick, online videos, simulations, motion capture and real robots. The baselines also differ: a fixed robot, cross-training, the best single policy, random assignment, turn-taking, an optimal agent. That makes a row-by-row ranking meaningless, so here is what each headline number measures.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>Testbed and humans</th>\n<th>What the headline number measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared autonomy</td>\n<td>Table clearing, 51 joystick users</td>\n<td>12 of 16 switched to the better goal at least once; trust above fixed robot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual adapt. '17</td>\n<td>Table carrying, 69 online users</td>\n<td>Share of people who adapted: 57% against 26% with cross-training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ABPS</td>\n<td>MORSE conveyor, simulated humans</td>\n<td>Discounted reward and warnings against the best single policy in hindsight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual adapt. '21</td>\n<td>Team Space Fortress, online humans</td>\n<td>Team score of adaptive agent against random partner assignment (the 42.3% baseline is unclear)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approx. Bayes</td>\n<td>UR5e and a human, 16-cube packing</td>\n<td>Task time in seconds against a turn-taking baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure antic.</td>\n<td>Simulated box moving, 40 runs per case</td>\n<td>Task success rate, 25% to 100% in the first case (a before/after pair; no other planner is compared)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HOTSPOT</td>\n<td>Predator-Prey sim, Toxic Waste robot</td>\n<td>Steps to finish against an optimal agent that knows the task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lead or follow</td>\n<td>48-person Fetch block task, plus simulation</td>\n<td>Agreement of estimates with interview groups; wrong human actions in simulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The human numbers deserve a second look. Nikolaidis' studies count how many people <em>changed their behaviour</em>; Li et al. measure team score in a game; Felip et al. measure seconds; Görür et al. and Izquierdo-Badiola et al. use simulated humans. Only the two Nikolaidis papers report trust scores in the notes I have. A reader who places \"36.49 s\" next to \"+39.2%\" or \"100%\" has compared things that have nothing in common. Within a row the comparisons are fair; across rows they are not, and the line has no shared benchmark.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-open-problems-and-a-reading-order\">Open problems, and a reading order</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-what-is-still-open\">What is still open</h3>\n<p>In rough order of how much they worry me:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Evidence with real people is thin.</strong> Several tests use simulated or online humans. HOTSPOT's real-robot trials used 7 lab members, Lead or follow's participants are mostly young students, and Approx. Bayes does not state its participant count. The larger studies are online (69, 104 and 134 players).</li>\n<li><strong>Traits are assumed fixed or slow.</strong> BAM fixes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> per person; Lead or follow assumes preference changes slowly; only ABPS tests a type switch (every 30 tasks, in simulation). The IJRR paper's own limit, that bounded memory may miss people who reflect on long histories, is untested here.</li>\n<li><strong>The state lists are fixed in advance.</strong> Five <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> levels, 16 hand-made types and 20 policies, 24 library policies, a task library, 11 trait values. Mutual adapt. '21 states the assumption outright: the library covers the human's way of playing. What happens when it does not is not measured.</li>\n<li><strong>The robot never errs.</strong> Failure antic. and Lead or follow state this, and no member relaxes it. That is a strong assumption for the physical robots the papers care about.</li>\n<li><strong>Only dyads.</strong> Every test pairs one person with one robot or agent; Lead or follow lists larger teams as future work. This is a long way from the open-team and multi-teammate variations that the AHT survey lists.</li>\n<li><strong>The person's adaptation is modelled, but the robot's effect on it is thin.</strong> BAM treats <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a fixed property of a person, yet in the repeated-trial study only 13% of non-adapters later switched. Whether a robot can change <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> over a long collaboration, rather than just estimate it, is not studied in this tree. This is my reading of the gap, not a claim from the papers.</li>\n<li><strong>No common benchmark, little cross-citation.</strong> The notes find only 7 member-to-member citations, and no paper compares itself against another member. Nobody has put, say, ABPS and Mutual adapt. '21 on the same task, which would be a cheap and informative study.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"b-if-you-only-have-an-afternoon\">If you only have an afternoon</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 · Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments\">Nikolaidis et al., IJRR 2017</button> for BAM and the guide-or-comply robot. If you read one paper, make it this one.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"70\" title=\"Shared autonomy · Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy\">Nikolaidis et al., HRI 2017</button> to see the same model with a disagreement penalty and a joystick.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"ABPS · Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types\">Görür et al., AAMAS 2019</button> for the type-and-policy-library alternative, read with the baseline in mind.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 · Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams\">Li et al., THMS 2021</button> to see how \"mutual adaptation\" can mean something quite different when no human model is learned.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"HOTSPOT · HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams\">Ribeiro et al., PLOS ONE 2024</button> for hidden-task inference and asking when unsure.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Lead or follow · To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Noormohammadi-Asl et al., T-RO 2025</button> to see the 2017 model reused, with its 48-person study.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"72\" title=\"Human modelling · Human modeling for human–robot collaboration\">Hiatt et al., IJRR 2017</button> as a reference map, skimmed; read <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"76\" title=\"Failure antic. · Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration\">Failure antic.</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Approx. Bayes · Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference\">Approx. Bayes</button> only if planning costs or reach prediction are your topic.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the ad hoc teamwork background, begin with <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"AHT position · Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination\">Stone et al. 2010</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"AHT survey · A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research\">Mirsky et al. 2022</button>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-how-these-numbers-were-handled\">How these numbers were handled</h3>\n<p>Every figure above comes from the paper it is attributed to, via my own notes on each paper. Approximate or read off charts: the bar counts for Mutual adapt. '17 (about 13/23 and 6/23) and HOTSPOT's Toxic Waste step counts. Derived: the \"about 10%\" for Approx. Bayes (my arithmetic). Unclear or contradictory in the source: the 42.3% in Mutual adapt. '21, the interview-group counts of Lead or follow, and the policy count in Mutual adapt. '21 (12 against 13 + 11). The venues of Mutual adapt. '21, Approx. Bayes and Lead or follow come from file names, not from the PDFs, and one inheritance edge (between the two mutual-adaptation papers) was added by me; the three-stage grouping is a reading order. Proofs were not checked, and human-study sizes are as reported.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-references\">References</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Stone et al., 2010. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Ad%20Hoc%20Autonomous%20Agent%20Teams%3A%20Collaboration%20without%20Pre-Coordination\">Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination</a>. <em>AAAI 2010</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Barrett et al., 2017. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Making%20friends%20on%20the%20fly%3A%20Cooperating%20with%20new%20teammates\">Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates</a>. <em>AIJ 2017</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"5\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Nikolaidis et al., 2017. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Human-Robot%20Mutual%20Adaptation%20in%20Shared%20Autonomy\">Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy</a>. <em>HRI 2017</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"70\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Nikolaidis et al., 2017. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Human-robot%20mutual%20adaptation%20in%20collaborative%20tasks%3A%20Models%20and%20experiments\">Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments</a>. <em>IJRR 2017</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"71\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Hiatt et al., 2017. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Human%20modeling%20for%20human%E2%80%93robot%20collaboration\">Human modeling for human–robot collaboration</a>. <em>IJRR 2017</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"72\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Görür et al., 2019. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Anticipatory%20Bayesian%20Policy%20Selection%20for%20Online%20Adaptation%20of%20Collaborative%20Robots%20to%20Unknown%20Human%20Types\">Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types</a>. <em>AAMAS 2019</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"73\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Li et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Individualized%20Mutual%20Adaptation%20in%20Human-Agent%20Teams\">Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams</a>. <em>IEEE THMS 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"57\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Mirsky et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A%20Survey%20of%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20Research\">A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research</a>. <em>EUMAS 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Felip et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Intuitive%20%26%20Efficient%20Human-robot%20Collaboration%20via%20Real-time%20Approximate%20Bayesian%20Inference\">Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference</a>. <em>IROS 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"75\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Izquierdo-Badiola et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Improved%20Task%20Planning%20through%20Failure%20Anticipation%20in%20Human-Robot%20Collaboration\">Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration</a>. <em>ICRA 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"76\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Ribeiro et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=HOTSPOT%3A%20An%20ad%20hoc%20teamwork%20platform%20for%20mixed%20human-robot%20teams\">HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams</a>. <em>PLOS ONE 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"86\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Noormohammadi-Asl et al., 2025. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=To%20Lead%20or%20to%20Follow%3F%20Adaptive%20Robot%20Task%20Planning%20in%20Human-Robot%20Collaboration\">To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration</a>. <em>T-RO 2025</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"87\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n</ol></div>");
